# How RunPod actually behaves, and where our deployment breaks.
# Grounded in scripts/{pod_up,provision_sm90,pod_run,pod_idle_stop}.sh and in the
# failures of 2026-08-06/07, which are annotated with what they cost.
# Render: d2 docs/runpod_deployment.d2 docs/runpod_deployment.svg

direction: down

title: |||md
  # RunPod deployment: the model, the money, and the failure modes

  Two ideas drive almost every mistake below.
  **(1) `desiredStatus: RUNNING` is a WISH, not an observation** — it is what we
  asked for, not what happened. Billing follows the wish; the container may never
  start. **(2) Only the network volume survives a stop.** Everything else is a
  consequence of those two.
||| {near: top-center}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ object model
model: "1 — What a pod IS" {
  style.stroke-dash: 3

  m: |||md
    ## Three nested things, each with its own failure mode

    | layer | we choose | it can fail by |
    |---|---|---|
    | **datacenter** | `--dataCenterId` | having no capacity for the GPU |
    | **machine** | *nothing* — RunPod picks | being in a maintenance window |
    | **container** | `--imageName` | never starting on that GPU family |

    We only ever control the ends. The MACHINE is assigned, which is why
    `pod_up.sh` polls `myself.pods.machine.maintenance*` before resuming: a pod
    started into a maintenance window bills while its volume is unreachable.
  |||
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ lifecycle
life: "2 — Lifecycle, and exactly where money starts" {
  direction: right

  created: "create accepted\npod id returned"
  billing: "**BILLING STARTS**\ndesiredStatus RUNNING" {style.fill: "#fdecea"; style.stroke: "#c0392b"}
  pulling: "image pull +\ncontainer start"
  live: "runtime != null\nruntime.ports populated" {style.fill: "#eaf5ea"}
  usable: "SSH answers\n= actually usable" {style.fill: "#eaf5ea"}

  created -> billing -> pulling -> live -> usable

  gap: |||md
    ## The gap that cost us $9

    Billing begins at **RUNNING**. Usability begins three states later. Nothing
    in the API forces the middle to ever complete, and `runpodctl pod list`
    reports `RUNNING` the whole time.

    On 2026-08-07 two H100 pods sat in that gap for **13 and 22 minutes** and
    never left it — `runtime: null`, `ports: []`, and the SSH proxy answering
    `container not found`. They billed the entire time.

    **The only honest readiness test is a command that runs ON the pod.**
    `desiredStatus` cannot distinguish "booting" from "never going to boot".
  ||| {style.fill: "#fdecea"; style.stroke: "#c0392b"}
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ storage
store: "3 — Storage: what survives what" {
  direction: right

  disk: |||md
    ### Container disk — `/root`
    Holds the synced repo (`/root/debate`), logs, run outputs.

    **WIPED on stop.** Destroyed on remove.
    Sized by `--containerDiskSize` (we use 200 GB).
  ||| {style.fill: "#fdecea"}

  vol: |||md
    ### Network volume — `/workspace`
    `$VOL` in every script. Holds `envs/verl-sm90` and `envs/verl-b200`,
    `hf/` (121 GB of weights), `uv/cache`, `wheels/`.

    **Survives stop AND remove.** Bills ~$0.07/GB/month while idle.
    Attached at create with `--networkVolumeId`; **cannot be added later**, and
    it is DATACENTER-LOCAL — only six datacenters offer both volumes and H200.

    **Reachable without any pod**, over S3:
    `https://s3api-<dc-lowercased>.runpod.io`, bucket = volume id, using S3
    credentials from the console (not the API key). Seed it, migrate it between
    regions, or pull checkpoints off it from a laptop. Renting a GPU to run
    `rsync` is paying a GPU rate to move bytes.
  ||| {style.fill: "#eaf5ea"}

  note: |||md
    Every run so far has had **no network volume**, so `/workspace` was just
    container disk. That single fact explains the ~80 min of cold provisioning
    on every pod, and why every stop had to be a remove preceded by a manual
    evacuation — an evacuation the S3 endpoint would have made unnecessary.
  ||| {style.fill: "#fff8e1"}
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ access
access: "4 — Two ways in, and they fail differently" {
  direction: right

  tcp: |||md
    ### Direct TCP
    `--ports '22/tcp'` -> `runtime.ports[]` gives `ip:publicPort`.

    Fast, supports `rsync`/`scp`. **Requires `runtime` to exist**, so it is
    useless in exactly the failure above.
    `scp` also silently fails host-key verification where `rsync -e ssh` with
    the right flags succeeds — checksum every transfer, never trust exit codes.
  |||

  proxy: |||md
    ### SSH proxy
    `<podId>-<hash>@ssh.runpod.io`, needs `--startSSH` and a PTY (`ssh -tt`).

    Works without any port mapping, which makes it the **diagnostic** channel:
    it distinguishes "no route" from `container not found`. That is how the
    2026-08-07 pods were finally diagnosed.
  |||
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ pipeline
pipe: "5 — Our script pipeline" {
  direction: down

  up: |||md
    **`pod_up.sh`** — RESUME only, no create path.
    Gates on maintenance windows (only visible on the authenticated GraphQL
    API; the public status page omits them). Exit 8 is the one that means money.
  |||
  prov: |||md
    **`provision_sm90.sh`** — builds `$VOL/envs/verl-sm90`.
    Joint vllm+verl resolve (~25 min), three source patches, flash-attn
    (**~45 min from source, ~1s with `FLASH_ATTN_WHEEL=`**), sanity import gate.
    Every step bounded by `timeout(1)`.
  |||
  run: |||md
    **`pod_run.sh`** — launch lock, trainer guard, judge vLLM (debate mode),
    then `exec`s the trainer and starts the idle watchdog.
    Model load + CUDA graph capture takes **~8 min before the first step line** —
    a trainer is not dead because its process is briefly absent.
  |||
  idle: |||md
    **`pod_idle_stop.sh`** — STOPS, never removes. Evacuates `/root` to
    `/workspace` first, because stop wipes the container disk.
    Destroying a pod stays a human decision.
  |||

  up -> prov -> run -> idle
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ failures
fail: "6 — Every failure we actually hit, and its fix" {
  f: |||md
    ## Observed, with cost

    | failure | root cause | fix |
    |---|---|---|
    | "no instances available" | no `--dataCenterId`; RunPod picked a region with no stock. **H200 was available in 7 DCs the whole time** | query `runpodctl datacenter list` first, create into a named DC |
    | pod bills, container never starts (**~$9**) | trusted `desiredStatus: RUNNING` | readiness gate: poll for `runtime.ports` **and** a successful remote command; auto-delete on deadline |
    | accidentally created 2 pods while "probing" | used `create` as a query | `datacenter list` / `gpu list` are the read-only probes |
    | 45 min rebuilding flash-attn | no wheel input | `FLASH_ATTN_WHEEL=` (landed), and a network volume |
    | ~80 min cold provisioning, every time | no network volume | attach one at create — **it cannot be added later** |
    | about to rebuild an env that already exists elsewhere | assumed a volume needs a pod to touch | it does not: S3 endpoint, no GPU, no hourly rate |
    | truncated wheel transfer; silent `scp` failure | trusted exit codes | `rsync` + sha256 both directions |
    | watcher declared a live run dead | process absence read as death | require a step line or 2 consecutive misses past an 8 min grace |
    | idle pod billing after a run | no watchdog armed | `pod_idle_stop.sh`, started by `pod_run.sh` |
    | 10-step arms overwrote each other's rollouts | shared output dir | per-run namespacing (landed in the merge) |
  ||| {style.fill: "#fdecea"}
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ proposal
fix: "7 — What we built in response" {
  p: |||md
    ## Three scripts, each closing one failure above

    **`pod_create.sh`** — creation with a bounded downside.
    Probe `datacenter list` (read-only) -> refuse unknown `(gpu, image)` pairs ->
    create into a NAMED datacenter with the volume attached -> **readiness gate**:
    poll until a port exists AND `ssh pod true` succeeds, deadline ~6 min, then
    **delete the pod**. That last step is the point: it turns an open-ended bill
    into a bounded one. Applied to 2026-08-07 it costs **$2 instead of $9**.
    A volume is datacenter-local, so its DC overrides the probe.

    **`pod_sync.sh`** — transfer, then prove it.
    rsync, then sha256 both ends and diff PER FILE. Copy exit status is not
    evidence: `scp` returned success having moved nothing, and a wheel arrived
    truncated at 57,446,400 of 62,109,228 bytes. Per-file because GNU
    `sha256sum` and BSD `shasum` disagree on path prefixes and sort order, so
    an aggregate hash false-alarms even when every file matches.

    **`pod_watch.sh`** — a verdict only when one is earned.
    Two consecutive misses AND past a 12-minute grace before believing a death;
    completion requires the step COUNT to reach target. Absence is not success.
    Model load plus graph capture runs ~8 min before the first step line, which
    is how a healthy run got declared dead at 60 seconds.
  ||| {style.fill: "#eaf5ea"}

  todo: |||md
    ## Still open

    **The volume exists** — `s3hz0k8153`, 1250 GB, **AP-IN-2**. It pins us to a
    datacenter that stocks H100 but no H200. Using it means H100; using H200
    means re-provisioning from scratch on every pod.

    **Why the `-cluster` image never started a container on H100 is unknown.**
    Both dead pods were created with NO `--dataCenterId`, so they landed
    wherever RunPod chose — the failure may have been a bad host rather than the
    GPU family. `pod_create.sh` refuses the pair rather than explaining it;
    settling it costs one bounded ~$2 attempt.
  ||| {style.fill: "#fff8e1"}
}

